Step-by-step algorithm for merging intervals:
Step 1: Sort all intervals based on their start times in ascending order.
Step 2: Initialize a list ans to store merged intervals.
Step 3: Set prev to the first interval in the sorted array.
Step 4: Traverse the rest of the intervals one by one (from the second interval onwards).
•	Sub-step 4a: Let curr be the current interval.
•	Sub-step 4b: Check if curr overlaps with prev (i.e., if curr[0] <= prev[1]).
o	If yes (overlap exists):
	Merge the intervals by updating prev[1] = max(prev[1], curr[1]).
o	If no (no overlap):
	Add prev to the result list ans.
	Set prev = curr.
Step 5: After the loop ends, add the last prev interval to ans.
Step 6: Convert the list ans to a 2D array and return it.
Time Complexity:
•	Sorting intervals → O(n log n)
•	Merging intervals → O(n)
Total = O(n log n)
Space Complexity:
•	Storing merged intervals in a list → O(n)
•	Temporary variables → O(1)
Total = O(n)



    -----------------> Code <---------------

class Solution {
    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a,b)-> Integer.compare(a[0],b[0]));

        List<int[]> ans=new ArrayList<>();
        int[] prev=intervals[0];

        int n=intervals.length;
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            int[] curr=intervals[i];
            if(curr[0]<=prev[1]){
                prev[1]=Math.max(prev[1],curr[1]);
            }else{
                ans.add(prev);
                prev=curr;
            }
        }

        ans.add(prev);
        return ans.toArray(new int[ans.size()][]);
    }
}
